items.workedExampleMarkdown:
Ashgrove's escalation form, with rules that depend on each other.

```js
// Per-field validation cannot express this rule at all.
function validatePhone(phone) {
  return phone ? null : 'Phone is required';   // wrong whenever contact is email
}
```

The rule is not 'phone is required'. It is 'phone is required *when contact method is phone*', and a validator that receives only the phone value has been handed too little information to decide.

```js
// Validity as one function of everything.
function errorsFor(values) {
  const errors = {};
  if (!values.reason) errors.reason = 'Choose a reason';
  if (values.reason === 'other' && values.detail.trim().length < 10) {
    errors.detail = 'Describe the problem in a little more detail';
  }
  if (values.contact === 'phone' && !values.phone) {
    errors.phone = 'Phone number is required for a phone callback';
  }
  return errors;
}
```

One function, all the rules, no hidden state - and testable without rendering anything, which is why this is the shape the exercises use.

Disclosure is layered on top rather than mixed in:

```js
const errors = errorsFor(values);
const shown = submitted ? errors : pick(errors, touched);
const canSubmit = Object.keys(errors).length === 0 && status !== 'submitting';
```

Note that `canSubmit` consults `errors`, not `shown`. Gating submission on the errors the user happens to have seen would let an untouched invalid field through - and gating disclosure on submission alone would ambush someone with six red messages the first time they click.

The trap this arrangement still has: if selecting email contact hides the phone field, `errors.phone` can persist while nothing on screen mentions it. Submission is then blocked by an error the user cannot see or fix. Clearing irrelevant values when the branch changes is the usual fix, and it has to be deliberate.

solution.code:
function escalationErrors(values) {
  const errors = [];

  if (values.reason.trim() === '') {
    errors.push('reason');
  }
  if (values.reason === 'other' && values.detail.trim().length < 10) {
    errors.push('detail');
  }
  if (values.contact !== 'email' && values.contact !== 'phone') {
    errors.push('contact');
  }
  if (values.contact === 'phone' && values.phone.trim() === '') {
    errors.push('phone');
  }
  if (values.dueDate === '' || values.dueDate < '2026-01-01') {
    errors.push('dueDate');
  }

  errors.sort();
  return errors;
}

