items.workedExampleMarkdown:
The windowing arithmetic, and the key mistake that survives it.

```js
// 50,000 rows, all of them created and reconciled on every render.
{tickets.map((t) => <Row key={t.id} ticket={t} />)}
```

```js
// Render the visible slice, and reserve the space the rest would occupy.
const first = Math.floor(scrollTop / ROW_HEIGHT);
const last = Math.ceil((scrollTop + viewportHeight) / ROW_HEIGHT);
const start = Math.max(0, first - OVERSCAN);
const end = Math.min(tickets.length, last + OVERSCAN);

<div style={{ height: tickets.length * ROW_HEIGHT }}>
  <div style={{ transform: `translateY(${start * ROW_HEIGHT}px)` }}>
    {tickets.slice(start, end).map((t) => <Row key={t.id} ticket={t} />)}
  </div>
</div>
```

The outer height keeps the scrollbar honest; the translate puts the rendered slice where it belongs. Around twenty-five rows exist at any moment regardless of whether the dataset holds fifty or fifty thousand.

The clamps are not decoration. Without `Math.max(0, ...)` a scroll position of zero with an overscan of five asks for row -5; without `Math.min(total, ...)` the end of the list requests rows past the data and renders blanks.

```jsx
// The key that makes an insertion look like an edit.
{tickets.map((t, i) => <Row key={i} ticket={t} />)}
```

Insert a ticket at the top and every subsequent index shifts. React compares old key 0 with new key 0, sees different content, and updates - then does the same for all the rest. Beyond the wasted work, any state inside a row stays with its index: a half-typed reply moves to the ticket above, which reads as data corruption to whoever is looking at it.

solution.code:
function visibleRange(scrollTop, rowHeight, viewportHeight, total, overscan) {
  if (total === 0) {
    return { start: 0, end: 0 };
  }
  const firstVisible = Math.floor(scrollTop / rowHeight);
  const lastVisible = Math.ceil((scrollTop + viewportHeight) / rowHeight);
  const start = Math.max(0, firstVisible - overscan);
  const end = Math.min(total, lastVisible + overscan);
  return { start: start, end: end };
}

grading.starterCode:
function visibleRange(scrollTop, rowHeight, viewportHeight, total, overscan) {
  // No overscan and no clamping.
  const start = Math.floor(scrollTop / rowHeight);
  const end = Math.floor((scrollTop + viewportHeight) / rowHeight);
  return { start: start, end: end };
}

