Ashgrove's ticket queue renders a windowed list. Agents report that when a new ticket arrives at the top, a reply they were part-way through typing appears on a different ticket.

```jsx
const { start, end } = visibleRange(scrollTop, ROW_HEIGHT, height, tickets.length, 5);

{tickets.slice(start, end).map((ticket, i) => (
  <TicketRow
    key={i}
    ticket={ticket}
    onSelect={() => select(ticket.id)}
  />
))}
```

`TicketRow` keeps the in-progress reply in its own `useState`. Select every accurate statement.

A ticket list of 8,000 rows is slow. Profiling shows:

- typing a single character in the filter box takes 900ms;
- the profiler attributes almost all of it to rendering `TicketRow`, 8,000 times;
- each individual `TicketRow` render is around 0.1ms;
- scrolling, once typing stops, is smooth.

Which change addresses the measured problem most directly?

items.workedExampleMarkdown:
The announcement, and the reconciliation.

```jsx
// Not announced: the region and its content arrive together.
{status === 'loading' && <div aria-live="polite">Loading tickets</div>}
```

Assistive technology watches an existing region for changes. Inserting the region and its text in the same update frequently produces no announcement at all - the region was never there to be watched.

```jsx
// Announced: the region is always present; only its text changes.
<div aria-live="polite" role="status">{announcement}</div>
```

with the message chosen as a summary rather than a running commentary:

```js
function announcementFor(state) {
  if (state.status === 'loading') return 'Loading tickets.';
  if (state.status === 'error') return 'Could not load tickets.';
  if (state.count === 0) return 'No tickets match your filters.';
  return `${state.count} tickets.`;
}
```

Then the optimistic half. The user marks a ticket resolved; the row updates immediately; the request is still in flight:

```js
function withPending(serverTickets, pending) {
  return serverTickets.map((ticket) =>
    pending[ticket.id] ? { ...ticket, status: pending[ticket.id] } : ticket,
  );
}
```

Two properties are doing the work. The server list is the source of truth for **which records exist** - a pending change for a ticket the server does not return is dropped, not resurrected, because the alternative is inventing a row that someone else may have deleted. And the pending map is consulted per record, so an unrelated ticket updated by a colleague arrives normally instead of being overwritten by our optimistic view of the world.

When the request fails, the entry is removed from `pending` - the row reverts by itself, because it was only ever a projection - and an `alert` region says so. Reverting silently is the failure mode to avoid: the user believes they resolved the ticket, and it is open.

solution.code:
function announcementFor(state) {
  if (state.status === 'loading') {
    return 'Loading tickets.';
  }
  if (state.status === 'error') {
    const base = 'Could not load tickets.';
    if (typeof state.message === 'string' && state.message.trim() !== '') {
      return base + ' ' + state.message.trim();
    }
    return base;
  }
  if (state.status === 'ready') {
    if (state.count === 0) {
      return 'No tickets match your filters.';
    }
    if (state.count === 1) {
      return '1 ticket.';
    }
    return state.count + ' tickets.';
  }
  return '';
}

grading.starterCode:
function announcementFor(state) {
  // Same sentence for every state, and no singular handling.
  return state.count + ' tickets.';
}

solution.code:
function withPending(serverTickets, pending) {
  const result = [];
  for (const ticket of serverTickets) {
    if (Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(pending, ticket.id)) {
      result.push({ id: ticket.id, status: pending[ticket.id] });
    } else {
      result.push(ticket);
    }
  }
  return result;
}

grading.starterCode:
function withPending(serverTickets, pending) {
  // Walks the pending map, so an unknown id is appended as a new ticket.
  const result = serverTickets.slice();
  for (const id of Object.keys(pending)) {
    result.push({ id: id, status: pending[id] });
  }
  return result;
}

